-              <p>For \(m &gt; n\), the triangle inequality gives \(|a_m - a_n| \le \sum_{k=n}^{m-1} |a_{k+1} - a_k| \le c\sum_{k=n}^{m-1} \beta^k\). The finite geometric sum formula of M1, which needs \(\beta \ne 1\), gives \(\sum_{k=n}^{m-1} \beta^k = \beta^n(1 - \beta^{m-n})/(1 - \beta) \le \beta^n/(1 - \beta)\), since \(0 &lt; \beta^{m-n} \le 1\). So \(|a_m - a_n| \le c\beta^n/(1 - \beta)\). Given \(\varepsilon &gt; 0\), choose \(N\) with \(c\beta^N/(1 - \beta) &lt; \varepsilon\), which exists because \(\beta^N \to 0\) for \(0 &lt; \beta &lt; 1\). Then \(|a_m - a_n| &lt; \varepsilon\) for \(m &gt; n \ge N\), and \((a_n)\) is Cauchy. Theorem 2.5 then gives convergence. The hypothesis of the formula is \(\beta \ne 1\), and it is satisfied here.</p>
+              <p>For \(m &gt; n\), the triangle inequality gives \(|a_m - a_n| \le \sum_{k=n}^{m-1} |a_{k+1} - a_k| \le c\sum_{k=n}^{m-1} \beta^k\). The finite geometric sum formula of M1 (Lesson 6, Theorem 2.2), which needs \(\beta \ne 1\), gives \(\sum_{k=n}^{m-1} \beta^k = \beta^n(1 - \beta^{m-n})/(1 - \beta) \le \beta^n/(1 - \beta)\), since \(0 &lt; \beta^{m-n} \le 1\). So \(|a_m - a_n| \le c\beta^n/(1 - \beta)\). Given \(\varepsilon &gt; 0\), choose \(N\) with \(c\beta^N/(1 - \beta) &lt; \varepsilon\), which exists because \(\beta^N \to 0\) for \(0 &lt; \beta &lt; 1\). Then \(|a_m - a_n| &lt; \varepsilon\) for \(m &gt; n \ge N\), and \((a_n)\) is Cauchy. Theorem 2.5 then gives convergence. The hypothesis of the formula is \(\beta \ne 1\), and it is satisfied here.</p>
